#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;

# PODNAME: simpici-dispatch
# ABSTRACT: Serve one restricted worker request over SSH standard input

use Carp qw( croak );
use Getopt::Long qw( GetOptions );
use JSON::MaybeXS;
use Path::Tiny qw( path );
use SimpiCI::Dispatcher;
use SimpiCI::Queue;
use SimpiCI::Store;

umask 0077;
my ($config_path, $worker);
GetOptions('config=s' => \$config_path, 'worker=s' => \$worker)
  && $config_path && $worker or croak 'Usage: simpici-dispatch --config FILE --worker NAME';
my $config = JSON::MaybeXS->new->decode(path($config_path)->slurp_utf8);
my $input = '';
alarm 30;
while (read(STDIN, my $chunk, 65536)) {
  $input .= $chunk;
  croak 'simpici-dispatch request too large' if length($input) > 8 * 1024 * 1024;
}
my $queue = SimpiCI::Queue->new(
  store => SimpiCI::Store->new(root => path($config->{root})),
  lease_seconds => ($config->{timeout} // 3600) + 1800
);
my $response = SimpiCI::Dispatcher->new(queue => $queue, config => $config)
  ->request($worker, JSON::MaybeXS->new->decode($input));
print JSON::MaybeXS->new(canonical => 1, convert_blessed => 1)->encode($response);

=head1 NAME

simpici-dispatch - restricted SSH dispatcher endpoint

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  simpici-dispatch --config /etc/simpici/dispatcher.json --worker vm1

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Install as an SSH forced command with restrict and no forwarding. Accepts one
size-limited JSON claim or finish request on standard input. The dispatcher
never checks out or executes repository code. Use a dedicated account.

The configuration is read for each request. A claim first checks every secret
grant of every repository; if one is unusable, the program exits nonzero with
a message naming the repository, the secret and the reason, and takes no
lease. A finish request is served without that check, so a completed run is
not lost to a configuration error.

A claim leaves the secret values it handed out in C<claims/E<lt>runE<gt>.json>
below the state root, in the clear, so that the log of the run can be
redacted when it arrives. The file is removed once the completion is accepted
and recorded. Every request, whichever it is and before anything else, removes
the files of runs whose lease expired or that are completed, including those
an earlier version left behind. A lease that expires while no worker connects
keeps its file until the next request. A completion that finds no file for a
run it can still complete is recorded with a log of one line saying that the
log was withheld, see L<SimpiCI::Dispatcher/Secret snapshot of a claim>.

A request that was served is answered with one JSON object on standard
output and the exit status 0. A completion that can never be accepted is
answered in that way too, with the reason as the only key:

  {"rejected":"expired claim"}

The reasons are C<unknown run>, C<stale claim> for a run claimed by another
worker name or token, and C<expired claim> for a lease that is over. The
worker takes this answer as final, stops sending the completion and keeps it
aside. Every other failure, such as a configuration that cannot be read, a
queue that cannot be locked or written, or a request that is not complete,
ends the program with a message on standard error and another exit status
than 0, and the worker sends the request again. An exit status alone never
says that a completion was refused: C<ssh> uses 255 for a connection it
could not make.

The clone URLs of the configuration are not checked here. This program reads
no remote, and a queued event passed L<SimpiCI::Event> when it was enqueued;
C<simpicid> refuses a clone URL with credentials before it polls.

=cut
